const CH8: CompactQuestion[] = [
  // Ch 8: Unity in Diversity, or 'Many in the One' (20 MCQs)
  [1, 1, 'What famous phrase summarizes India’s remarkable blend of varied languages, cuisines, textiles, and shared cultural bonds?', 'Division in Distance', 'Unity in Diversity ("Many in the One")', 'Strict Uniformity Only', 'Isolated Islands', 'B', 'Countless diverse traditions coexist harmoniously inside one connected cultural tapestry.', 'factual'],
  [1, 1, 'How does a humble staple ingredient like **Rice** or **Lentils/Spices** prove India’s "Unity in Diversity" across kitchens from Kashmir to Kanyakumari?', 'Only Delhi eats lentils', 'Practically every Indian region uses shared core grains and spices (rice, wheat, pulses, turmeric, cumin) yet weaves them creatively into distinct regional dishes—idli, biryani, khichdi, pitha, pongal!', 'Every state imports plastic food', 'All states cook exact identical curry recipes', 'B', 'Common culinary foundations blossom into dazzling regional variety.', 'conceptual'],
  [1, 2, 'Give one colourful example of a single traditional garment (like the unstitched length of cloth—Sari or Dhoti) demonstrating unity across variety:', 'Saris only exist in factories abroad', 'An identical concept of draped graceful woven cloth spans Banarasi silk, Kanjeevaram, Chanderi, Patola, Pochampally, Assam Muga, and Sambalpuri across hundreds of styles and weaving idioms!', 'All states ban cotton saris', 'Only winter wool is worn nationwide', 'B', 'Shared textile grammar paired with distinctive local motifs binds artisan regions together.', 'factual'],
  [1, 2, 'How do seasonal harvest festivals celebrated around mid-January (Makar Sankranti, Pongal, Lohri, Magh Bihu, Uttarayan) reflect cultural unity?', 'Each celebrates snowstorms only', 'Across vastly different languages and names from Punjab and Gujarat to Tamil Nadu and Assam, communities rejoice together at the Sun’s northward shift (*Uttarayana*) and winter harvest!', 'None involve sweet dishes', 'Festivals fall strictly in August', 'B', 'One seasonal agricultural rhythm inspires joyful regional variations nationwide.', 'conceptual'],
  [2, 1, 'Why did famous folklorist K.S. Singh’s Anthropological Survey "People of India" project discover astonishing genetic and cultural overlapping across regions?', 'Cities fenced borders completely', 'Centuries of pilgrimage, river commerce, artisans’ travel, and migration wove regional communities into shared linguistic and cultural kinship!', 'Trains operated in 500 BCE', 'People never travelled anywhere', 'B', 'Mobility across India historically nurtured mutual borrowing and kinship.', 'factual'],
  [2, 1, 'How do India’s great ancient epics—the **Ramayana** and the **Mahabharata**—connect folk traditions even across hills, islands, and tribal regions?', 'Only one city owns printed copies', 'Countless regional retellings (Valmiki, Kamban in Tamil, Krittibas in Bengali, Tulsidas in Awadhi, plus Bhil, Gond, and Northeast folk versions!) celebrate local landscapes where heroes allegedly paused!', 'Nobody outside cities heard them', 'Epics ignore forests and rivers', 'B', 'Reimagined proudly in hundreds of local tongues and tribal art forms, epics unite local geography with shared stories.', 'conceptual'],
  [2, 2, 'Explain what rich woven Indian **chintz** cotton textiles historically meant in global trade before industrial mills:', 'Heavy wool rugs only', 'Colourfast vividly printed Indian cottons became so wildly prized worldwide that European powers later tried banning them just to protect local textile makers!', 'Cheap paper clothes', 'Plastic raincoats', 'B', 'Indian weavers’ natural dye mastery shaped world fashion and trade.', 'factual'],
  [2, 2, 'Why must celebrating "Unity in Diversity" reject forcing every community to dress, speak, and eat identically?', 'Because identical uniforms run out of dyes', 'Forced uniformity erases vibrant unique heritage and breeds resentment; genuine unity flourishes when diverse expressions respect and enrich one another freely!', 'Festivals become too cheap', 'Maps look duller', 'B', 'Harmonious pluralism treats differences as mutual wealth, never threats.', 'conceptual'],
  [3, 1, 'Suppose a school celebrates **Matribhasha Diwas** (Mother Language Day) where students greet "Good Morning" and share one proverb in 12 home languages (Bhojpuri, Marathi, Odia, Tamil, Khasi, Dogri, Malayalam...). How does this simple morning enrich unity?', 'Wastes 10 quiet math minutes', 'Hearing each other’s home rhythms nurtures mutual respect, curiosity, and pride—proving many linguistic branches grow cleanly on one common tree!', 'Confuses dictionaries forever', 'Proves English alone matters', 'B', 'Linguistic inclusion builds warm belonging inside classrooms.', 'application'],
  [3, 1, 'Compare the culinary roles of **turmeric, ginger, cardamom, and black pepper** across North, South, East, and West Indian kitchens:', 'Only coastal fishermen know spices', 'Shared indigenous medicinal spices travel across all regions—used gently in Gujarati dals, Bengali panch phoron, Kashmiri kahwa, and Kerala rasam!', 'Spices turn food toxic in plains', 'Farmers never share seeds', 'B', 'Spices weave everyday household health and cultural memory nationwide.', 'factual'],
  [3, 2, 'Why did folk artists like Panchatantra and Jataka storytellers help bridge boundaries silently across Asia as well as India?', 'Tales needed passports', 'Clever universal animal fables about friendship, greed, and prudence delighted villagers, traders, and scholars equally across languages!', 'Merchants banned poems', 'Printed newspapers existed in 200 BCE', 'B', 'Timeless moral humour travels effortlessly across cultural boundaries.', 'conceptual'],
  [3, 2, 'Identify the FALSE statement about India’s Unity in Diversity:', 'Over 1,300 mother tongues and scores of dance, music, and weaving traditions enrich Indian life', 'Natural grains like millets, rice, and pulses appear in distinct regional recipes from Himachal to Tamil Nadu', 'True national unity requires wiping out local mother tongues, regional dances, and traditional crafts so everyone looks identical', 'Harvest festivals like Pongal, Bihu, Lohri, and Makar Sankranti share joyful seasonal roots', 'C', 'Preserving regional variety strengthens rather than weakens Indian unity!', 'conceptual'],
  [4, 1, 'Why did India’s Constitution makers establish a rich list of 22 Scheduled Languages (Eighth Schedule) alongside encouraging preservation of hundreds of indigenous tongues?', 'To discourage citizens from talking to neighbours', 'Respecting citizens’ mother tongues affirms human dignity and deepens participatory democracy across states!', 'Because one alphabet failed completely', 'Only politicians speak multiple languages', 'B', 'Constitutional pluralism institutionalizes linguistic respect cleanly.', 'application'],
  [4, 1, 'Notice how classical **Kanjeevaram**, **Banarasi**, **Pochampally Ikat**, and **Chanderi** handlooms differ in motif yet share identical patient shuttle craftsmanship: What economic threat faces diverse heritage handlooms today?', 'Silkworms stopped spinning cleanly', 'Cheap fast-fashion powerloom imitations mimic traditional weaves cheaply—threatening artisan livelihoods unless consumers consciously value authentic handloom craft!', 'Customers stopped wearing clothing', 'Cotton vanished completely', 'B', 'Supporting handloom artisans sustains living cultural diversity cleanly.', 'application'],
  [4, 2, 'How did ancient pilgrim trails connecting Badrinath, Rameswaram, Puri, Dwarka, and Kamakhya turn ordinary medieval villagers into fellow citizens mentally long before railways?', 'Trains ran hourly in 900 CE', 'Travelers walking between distant sacred sites exchanged songs, seeds, crafts, recipes, and languages—knitting geographic kinship across the whole subcontinent!', 'Kings paid airfares', 'Rivers froze into highways', 'B', 'Sacred geography forged living networks across distant corners of Bharat.', 'conceptual'],
  [4, 2, 'What makes "Many in the One" (*Ekam sat...* / Mosaic metaphor) cleaner than a "Melting Pot" metaphor when describing Indian society?', 'Melting pots erase all spices completely into tasteless mush', 'In a vibrant **Thali** or **Mosaic**, each distinct dish or tile keeps its unique aroma, texture, and heritage while together composing one balanced nourishing whole!', 'Melting pots require fire heaters', 'Mosaics break easily', 'B', 'Unity does not demand erasure of distinct regional identity.', 'conceptual'],
  [5, 1, 'Assertion (A): Sharing meals, learning greetings in a neighbour’s language, and celebrating diverse regional festivals inside schools builds stronger civic solidarity than any dry lecture alone. Reason (R): Direct joyful experience of cultural diversity dissolves unfamiliar prejudice and builds lasting mutual empathy among young citizens.', 'Both A and R are true, and R explains A', 'Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A', 'A is true, R is false', 'A is false, R is true', 'A', 'Experiential respect makes pluralism feel warmly natural.', 'assertion-reason'],
  [5, 1, 'Assertion (A): If a student sees an unfamiliar regional dance or food from another state, laughing dismissively protects cultural pride. Reason (R): Curiosity and respectful appreciation recognize that unfamiliar traditions carry centuries of regional ecology and artistry.', 'Both A and R are true', 'A is true, R is false', 'A is false, R is true', 'Both A and R are false', 'C', 'Cultural humility turns unfamiliarity into joyful discovery.', 'assertion-reason'],
  [5, 2, 'Case Study: While preparing an "Ek Bharat Shreshtha Bharat" paired-state showcase between Rajasthan and Assam, a Class 6 team wants to highlight shared threads WITHOUT erasing unique identity. Which pairing shines brightest?', 'Presenting identical pizza stalls only', 'Exhibiting Rajasthan’s colourful block-printed cotton & Kalbelia rhythm alongside Assam’s delicate Muga silk & Bihu spring harvest joy—plus showing how Makar Sankranti & Magh Bihu celebrate the exact same harvest turn together!', 'Skipping folk songs totally', 'Showing black-and-white highway maps only', 'B', 'Revealing parallel rhythms across distinctive regional treasures brings "Many in the One" alive!', 'case-based'],
  [5, 2, 'Case Study: Why do Indian botanical gardens and spice markets remind historians that our culinary "diversity" blends indigenous gifts (black pepper, ginger, turmeric, mango) peacefully with ancient exchanges (potato, tomato, chilli from Americas after 1500s)?', 'Chillies grew on Arctic icebergs', 'Living culture welcomes helpful new ingredients creatively without forgetting native roots—adapting external ideas warmly into uniquely Indian expressions!', 'Every spice vanished in 1800', 'Tomatoes originated in Delhi', 'B', 'Confident open cultures synthesize new contributions joyfully over centuries.', 'case-based'],
];

